# Code style

## Always brace `if` bodies

Never put an `if` body on the same line without braces. Always use `{ }`, with the body on its own line. This applies to `else`, `for`, and `while` too.

```ts
// Bad
if (!text || sending) return;

// Good
if (!text || sending) {
  return;
}
```

## Separate conceptually distinct blocks with a blank line

Put a blank line between groups of statements that do different things, for example: input validation, setting up state, the main work, and building the result. Keep statements that belong to one step together, without blank lines between them.

```ts
// Bad
const text = content.trim();
if (!text) {
  return;
}
const started = performance.now();
setSending(true);
const response = await fetch("/api/chat", { method: "POST", body });

// Good
const text = content.trim();
if (!text) {
  return;
}

const started = performance.now();
setSending(true);

const response = await fetch("/api/chat", { method: "POST", body });
```

## Document every function

Every named function needs a comment directly above it: function declarations, named function expressions, class methods, and function components. Say what it does, what each input means, and what it returns or changes. A few lines is enough. Skip anonymous callbacks passed to `map`, `filter`, `forEach`, and event handlers.

Do not restate the types. Say what the values mean, and for a `void` function say what it writes, renders, or sends.

```ts
// Bad: nothing above the function.
export async function searchPassages(question: string, k: number): Promise<Passage[]> {

// Good
// Find the passages closest in meaning to a question.
// question: the user's question. It is embedded with Voyage before the search.
// k: how many passages to return.
// Returns those passages, closest first, with their text and document details.
